# Patch: bf16 dense-GEMM tuned table for TP=4
#
# Base:   aiter @ d9e5ef7ce08ee7045d583aed768cff41aa9210fe (/sgl-workspace/aiter),
#         which is what SGLang 0.5.17 in this container imports.
# Apply:  cd /sgl-workspace/aiter && git apply /path/to/gemm_tuned_table_tp4.patch
#         then  rm -rf /tmp/aiter_configs   (the merged table is cached there)
#         and restart the server -- aiter reads the table once, at import.
#
# What it is
#   aiter looks up every bf16 dense GEMM in a CSV keyed on
#   (gfx, cu_num, M, N, K, bias, dtype, outdtype, scaleAB, bpreshuffle) and falls back to a
#   generic torch/hipblaslt call on a miss. The table AMD ships for this model,
#   model_configs/qwen3_5_397b_bf16_tuned_gemm.csv, was tuned at TP=2: its rows are
#   N in {10240, 8704, 8192, 4096} with K in {8192, 4096, 512}. At TP=4 every one of those
#   N and K halves, so not one row matches and every dense GEMM in the model misses.
#   This adds a TP=4 table for the M tiers the frozen workload actually runs:
#   M=64 (decode batch) and M=8192 / M=16384 (chunked prefill, chunked-prefill-size 16384).
#
#   Tuned with aiter's own tuner:
#     python3 /sgl-workspace/aiter/csrc/gemm_a16w16/gemm_a16w16_tune.py \
#       --input_file <shapes>.csv --tuned_file <out>.csv --libtype all \
#       --with-hipblaslt --splitK --compare --update_improved --min_improvement_pct 3
#   Per-shape kernel-level results are in analysis/bf16_gemm/tune.log and tune_prefill.log;
#   the largest are 8192x512x4096 52.45 -> 44.38 us (1.18x) and
#   16384x4608x4096 519.65 -> 443.71 us (1.17x).
#
#   The two deletions in dsv4_ and glm5_ are not optional. aiter merges every file under
#   model_configs/ into one table and hard-errors if two files key the same shape; those two
#   models had already been tuned at three shapes that TP=4 Qwen3.5-397B also hits, and the
#   new rows are faster at all three (45.76->39.36, 68.36->64.19, 212.35->210.23 us).
#   aiter's auto-resolver keeps the faster row and rewrites both files wholesale; deleting the
#   three lines by hand gets the same table with a three-line diff.
#
# Measured  (ISL 8192 / OSL 1024 / concurrency 64 / 192 prompts / TP=4; one server start per
#            arm, first benchmark after each start discarded)
#
#   baseline (frozen config, 8 warm runs / 3 starts)      2505.96 tok/s
#   + this patch          (5 warm runs / 1 start)         2546.04 tok/s   +1.60%
#   + topk_softmax_ldg_width.patch on top
#                         (8 warm runs / 2 starts)        2569.26 tok/s   +2.53%
#
#   Against the 2490.308 tok/s in the task document the stacked pair is +3.17%.
#   mean TTFT 5039.9 -> 5001.6 ms, mean TPOT 20.63 -> 20.04 ms (both improve).
#   gsm8k 5-shot exact_match strict-match 0.9765 +/- 0.0042 on the stacked pair against a
#   0.9691 gate and a 0.9773 baseline -- one answer of 1319. Reduction order in the dense
#   bf16 GEMMs is the only thing in this pair that can move an answer; the other half of the
#   stack is bit-identical.
#
#   Per-run numbers, arm tables and the noise floor are in FINDINGS.md section 6.
#   Raw benchmark output: results/gemm_only_w{1..5}_*, results/final_w{1..5}_*,
#   results/gemm_topk_w{1..3}_*, baseline results/baseC_w{1..3}_*.
#   Accuracy: eval_results/gemm_topk_gsm8k_20260820_133923 and eval_results/final_gsm8k_20260820_142652
#   -- two server instances, both 0.9765 (1288/1319), but not on the same 1288 questions: 4 flip
#   each way between two runs of the identical build, so the 1-answer gap to baseline is under the
#   stack's own run-to-run nondeterminism.
#
#   This patch stands alone. topk_softmax_ldg_width.patch also stands alone; they were
#   measured stacked and then separated by re-running this one on its own, so the +0.91%
#   attributed to the other patch is a difference, not an independent measurement.
#
diff --git a/aiter/configs/model_configs/qwen3_5_397b_bf16_tuned_gemm_tp4.csv b/aiter/configs/model_configs/qwen3_5_397b_bf16_tuned_gemm_tp4.csv
